2025-04-09 11:10:44 - scrape_sites - WARNING - Missing URL in link: {'_key': '35986ff4-8d56-4e61-99f7-f728ee07a077', '_id': 'Documents/35986ff4-8d56-4e61-99f7-f728ee07a077', '_rev': '_h2HDnfS---', 'data': {'url': 'https://smus.com/visual-chronology-science-discovery/', 'favicon': 'https://smus.com/static/icons/favicon-32x32.png', 'site_name': 'Visual Chronology of Science & Discovery', 'type': 'article', 'description': 'As Newton wrote, “If I have seen further it is by standing on the shoulders of giants”. But whose giant shoulders did Newton stand on? And did those giants stand on the shoulders of other giants? And how about Newton’s successors, or people working in other fields? As far as I can tell, it’s giants all the way down.   Last year, I got my hands on a remarkable book,  Asimov’s Chronology of Science and Discovery . It inspired me to produce a visual summary of human ingenuity, to see what one giant saw from the shoulders of another. After some experimentation, I turned it into an interactive visualization. You can play with it  here :', 'image': 'https://smus.com/static/icons/apple-touch-icon.png', 'title': 'Visual Chronology of Science & Discovery'}, 'modDate': '2024-05-16T16:39:13.684Z', 'createDate': '2024-05-16T16:39:13.684Z'}
